### Q4 — Customers who spent above the average customer spend
*(CTE for per-customer totals, then filter against the average)*

```sql
WITH customer_totals AS (
  SELECT c.customer_id, c.customer_name,
         SUM(oi.quantity * p.price) AS total_spent
  FROM customers c
  JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
  JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id
  JOIN products p ON p.product_id = oi.product_id
  GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.customer_name
)
SELECT customer_name, total_spent
FROM customer_totals
WHERE total_spent > (SELECT AVG(total_spent) FROM customer_totals)
ORDER BY total_spent DESC;
```

**What it answers:** Identifies above-average-value customers — the CTE `customer_totals` computes each customer's lifetime spend once, and the outer query filters against the average of that same CTE, rather than recalculating totals inline.

**Result:**

| customer_name | total_spent |
|---|---|
| Alice Uwase | 56000 |
| David Habimana | 33900 |

*(Average customer spend across all 5 buying customers: **32,860**. Alice and David are the only two above it.)*

